{"type": "text", "timestamp": 1790919065389, "sessionID": "ses_f04e9cd96ffehDyI8e1yilvE6q", "part": {"id": "prt_0fb17ff8e001eTE5W9VAqXuO0C_text-0", "sessionID": "ses_f04e9cd96ffehDyI8e1yilvE6q", "messageID": "msg_0fb17ff8e001eTE5W9VAqXuO0C", "type": "text", "text": "The evidence is conclusive from the analysis:\n\n- **Pure grayscale, zero color** \u2014 rules out *Existenz* (1999) and points to a B&W film from 1925\u20131968.\n- **Extremely dark throughout**: mean stripe luminance is only ~36/255, and **no stripe anywhere exceeds 87/255** \u2014 not one bright spot in the entire film. Only 72 of 1000 stripes even reach 60. This is the hallmark of *Night of the Living Dead*, an independent production notoriously shot at night with minimal lighting \u2014 most of the frame is in near-darkness for most of its runtime.\n- **Structure fits perfectly**:\n  - A modestly brighter patch ~5\u201312% in: the overcast daylight graveyard/driving scenes at the start (\"They're coming to get you, Barbara\").\n  - Slightly brighter bumps around ~55% and ~75\u201380%: the fire-burning scenes and the house lights during the zombie siege.\n  - A small lift at ~95\u201397%: the dawn posse footage with trucks.\n  - The final ~2.5% is **near-pure black** (mean 9/255): the film ends on grainy still photos over black, then the end title.\n\nEvery other B&W candidate (*Metropolis*, *Joan of Arc*, *Potemkin*, *His Girl Friday*, *The Lady Vanishes*, *The 39 Steps*) contains substantial bright-white sequences \u2014 stadium lights, sunlit Odessa steps, pale high-key close-ups, snowy exteriors, a brightly lit newsroom \u2014 which would show up as near-white stripes. *The Last Man on Earth* and *House on Haunted Hill* also have bright daylight/well-lit interior stretches and brighter endings. A barcode with literally no stripe brighter than ~34% gray, ending in black, is uniquely *Night of the Living Dead*.\n\nAnswer: C", "time": {"start": 1790919049106, "end": 1790919065389}}}
